Z||000000||Ronald Vodraska||06-20-2001||09:29 PM||||To air condition or not to air condition, that is the question.  The compressor is lying on the bottom of the engine compartment and I cannot get an answer from the previous owner as to whether the air conditioning worked.  For the sake of simplicity and ease of working on the car,  I am prone to rip the whole system out unless somebody says STOP.  Should I or shouldn't I?  Is it worth repairing and maintaining?  Or replace? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||06-20-2001||10:15 PM||||I surely wouldn't take the a/c out of my car without giving it a shot to see if it works at least... People don't always trust the R134 conversion kits, but for $30 and a little work you can find out if your a/c will work. If you find that it don't work, then you can rip it out and it won't have cost you all that much.<P>Personally, I'd just get another belt and bypass the compressor if my a/c wouldn't work after the conversion. The small amount of wait that the compressor adds will not effect the car all that much, and who knows, the day may come when you do want the a/c to work. <P>You know they are predicting global warming. Air conditioning could come in handy.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||06-21-2001||02:41 AM||||The simplist way I found to find leaks with out expensive tools is pump up the system with air. I use nitrogen but shop air will work just fine. To around 250 psi or so then spray down all the fittings and stuff with soapy water.||||reg||1
